# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Line ending policy
#
# This project is written on Windows and deployed on Raspberry Pi OS.
# Windows ends lines with CRLF, Linux with LF. Left unmanaged, the choice
# is made by each developer's personal Git config, so the same file can be
# committed two different ways and every line shows as changed.
#
# Policy: LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every
# platform. Windows tooling (Qt Creator, VS Code, MSVC, CMake, Git Bash)
# reads LF without complaint. Only Windows-native scripts keep CRLF.
#
# This file overrides core.autocrlf, so the rule travels with the repo
# instead of depending on how each machine happens to be configured.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Default for anything Git detects as text.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Scripts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shell scripts run on the Pi. A CRLF here makes the kernel read the
# shebang as "/bin/bash\r" and fail with a confusing "bad interpreter"
# error, so these must stay LF.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# PowerShell and batch files only ever run on Windows. Keep them native.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# ── Binary ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Marked explicitly so Git never attempts line ending conversion on them,
# which would corrupt the file.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
